--TEST--
`Core\Queue::push` refuses a `secret` in its `arguments` payload, bare and inside the literal written at the call, and the help names configuration as the place a job reads a credential from — `rule:concurrency/a-payload-refuses-secret-and-keeps-its-qualifiers`, `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s serialiser bullet says an encoded
// document is on its way to a response, a log or a queue. This is the queue
// spelled out: `push` encodes `arguments` into a durable row with the same encoder
// `Core\Json::encode` uses, and a worker process decodes it later, so the
// refusal is the encoder's and reports the encoder's code.
//
// It is a call-site rule for `Core\Log::write`'s reason twice over: `arguments` is
// declared `mixed`, which a `secret string` satisfies, and the bag itself
// arrives as the registry's own shape, so the written option is the last place
// the qualifier is visible at all.
secret string $token = "literal";
string $to = "a@b";

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. Nothing below runs — a case with an error
// section never reaches its output — so these are compile-time claims only.
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {arguments: ["to" => $to]});
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {queue: "mail"});

// The bare payload.
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {arguments: $token});

// And the shape the rule exists for: one credential among public fields, in
// both container spellings. The literal keeps the `secret` element on its own
// type, so the refusal is reported once, at the whole `arguments` value.
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {arguments: ["to" => $to, "token" => $token]});
Core\Queue::push("send-mail.nvs", {arguments: {to: $to, token: $token}});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0791]: a `secret` value cannot be passed to `Core\Queue::push`. The `arguments` payload is stored in the jobs table, and a worker reads it later
   --> case.nvs:22:47
%A
   = help: read the credential from configuration when the job runs. If one field must travel with the job, reveal that field with `Core\Secret::reveal(..., "reason")`
%A
